A savepoint is a named marker set at some point inside an active transaction, letting you later roll back just to that marker instead of undoing the entire transaction from the start. A full `ROLLBACK` undoes every change made since the transaction began (BEGIN/START TRANSACTION), ending the transaction entirely. `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT <name>` instead undoes only the changes made after that specific savepoint was established, while the transaction itself remains open - the savepoint stays valid and can be rolled back to again, and everything before that savepoint (including any earlier savepoints) is preserved. This is useful for handling errors partway through a complex, multi-step transaction: instead of scrapping all your work and restarting the whole transaction if one sub-step fails, you can roll back only that sub-step and continue from the savepoint. Rolling back to a savepoint implicitly destroys any savepoints established after it; `RELEASE SAVEPOINT` explicitly removes a savepoint without undoing the work done after it, once you're confident you no longer need to roll back to that point.
Example:
```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
SAVEPOINT after_debit;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2; - suppose this fails a business check
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT after_debit; - undo only the credit, keep the debit - retry the credit logic differently, then COMMIT;
```
